  "query_preview": "..\nCurrently there is lingering mid level clouds across Southeast\nArizona, with no precipitation or lightning differing from\nprevious nights. Clouds will linger around through the morning,\nbut should allow for atmospheric mixing and surface heating this\nafternoon and evening. \n\nToday continues to look like an active monsoon day, with much of\nSoutheast Arizona having chances for thunderstorms. The ridge of\nof high pressure is currently over central Arizona and will\ncontinue to elongate westward, keeping the upper level flow\nNE/E-ly with a modest amount of wind shear (15-25 kts) to help \nkeep thunderstorms a tad more longer lived and possibly produce\nsevere hail (1 inch in diameter). With this flow pattern, we \ncan start to see storm initiation in the White Mountains and \nSky Islands around noon then slowly move into the valleys around\n2 PM. Storms then continue across the area though the afternoon\nand evening. The thunderstorms continue to have more of a \nwind/blowing dust threat areawide. The 03/00Z HREF is \nhighlighting much of the area with 10 percent chances for 50 kts\nwithin 40 km, with a small 30 percent contour as well, \nindicating that any storm can be capable of producing severe \nwind gusts (58+ mph) and lofting up areas of blowing dust. \nThere is a potential for the formation of a haboob, especially \nmoving southwest to west across Cochise County based on the \ndirection of the storm movement and dust sources like the \nLordsburg Playa on NM and Willcox Playa. With this, a Blowing \nDust Advisory is in effect from 2 PM to 8 PM for areas Tucson \neastward, then as storms move west in effect in the Western \nDeserts 4 PM to 10 PM. Flash flooding is an added concern for \nareas south of Tucson in Santa Cruz, southeastern Pima, and \nsouthwestern Cochise counties with a Flash Flood Watch in effect\nfrom 2 PM to 10 PM today. The 03/00Z HREF is highlighting these\nareas with locally heavy rainfall with accumulations between 1 \nto 3 inches of rainfall in these areas (as well as in Sonora \nMexico). This rainfall can lead to flash flooding of roadways, \nnormally dry washes, rivers, creeks, and low laying areas. \nNogales Wash and the Santa Cruz River should be monitored for \nincreased flows. All this to say, the SPC has highlighted the \narea for marginal risk (5%) severe wind and hail for the central\nportion of the CWA and the WPC has highlighted the area for a \nslight risk (15%) for flash flooding for areas south of Tucson \nalong the International Border.\n\nThe high pressure continues to keep the easterly flow aloft, \nallowing for the area to clear out and allow for the atmosphere \nto recharge for increased thunderstorm activity through at least\nmid this week. The good news with the increasing storm coverage\nand activity, the Extreme Heat will begin to wain after today\nwhere temperatures will 'cool' to 3 to 6 degrees above normal\nwith Minor to Moderate HeatRisk the rest of the week.",
